from pathlib import Path
import sys,math
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('16');add=A.add
add('4.1-01','A pulse is a single disturbance that propagates.','It differs from a continuing periodic train of disturbances.','One quick movement of a rope end produces a pulse. Repeated movements produce a wave train. The disturbance transfers energy while the rope elements mainly move about their equilibrium positions rather than travelling with the pulse.','Define a pulse.','A pulse is a single propagating disturbance.')
add('4.1-02','An oscillation is repeated motion to and fro about an equilibrium position.','One complete cycle returns to the same state of position and direction.','A pendulum, tuning fork and stretched string can oscillate. The school syllabus groups simple oscillations here; the stricter term simple harmonic motion additionally requires a particular restoring-effect relation, so not every possible repeated motion has that special form. Count full cycles rather than one outward journey.','Define an oscillation.','It is a repeated to-and-fro motion about an equilibrium position.')
add('4.1-03','Wave propagation transfers energy from place to place without net transport of the medium in the basic wave model.','The disturbance travels while individual medium particles oscillate locally.','Mark a point on a rope: its motion can be up and down while the pulse travels horizontally. A floating marker can similarly reveal local surface motion in a water-wave demonstration. Real water can have currents as well, but bulk current is a separate effect from the ideal wave description.','Explain how a rope wave transfers energy without carrying the rope along its length.','Neighbouring rope elements interact and pass the disturbance onward.~Each element oscillates around its local position instead of travelling with the wave.')
add('4.1-04','Wave speeds depend on wave type and medium.','Useful approximate values are sound: air 340 m/s, water 1500 m/s, glass 5000 m/s; light: vacuum or air 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, water 2.25 × 10⁸ m/s, typical glass 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s.','These are representative school values, not exact constants for every sample. Temperature affects sound speed and glass composition affects both sound and light speeds. Use a supplied value in a calculation. Sound and light respond differently to the same medium, so their speed ordering must not be transferred from one type to the other.','State a typical speed of sound in air at ordinary room conditions.','Approximately 340 m/s.')
add('4.1-05','Sound often travels faster in solids than in gases because their mechanical response transmits disturbances more rapidly.','Close spacing and strong particle interactions in a typical solid promote rapid transfer of a compression disturbance.','Spacing alone is not a complete speed law: stiffness and inertia both matter. The qualitative particle picture compares typical glass with air rather than claiming every solid has an identical speed. Particles do not themselves travel from source to listener at the quoted wave speed.','Explain why sound typically travels faster in glass than in air.','The closely coupled solid particles pass mechanical disturbances rapidly.~The gas’s widely separated particles and different mechanical response transmit the disturbance more slowly.')
add('4.1-06','Light travels much faster than sound in air.','For a distant event, the visual signal generally arrives well before its sound.','A flash and thunder are produced during the same lightning event, but the sound takes appreciably longer to reach a distant observer. The delay estimates distance using sound speed when the light travel time is negligible at this scale. This is a propagation-time model, not an instruction to remain outside during a storm.','Explain why a distant lightning flash is normally seen before its thunder is heard.','The light and sound travel toward the observer at very different speeds.~Light arrives much earlier because its air speed is far greater.',heading='Different arrival times of light and sound')
add('4.1-07','A transverse disturbance oscillates perpendicular to propagation; a longitudinal disturbance oscillates parallel to it.','A rope wave is a transverse example and sound in air is longitudinal.','Longitudinal motion produces compressions and rarefactions rather than a literal up-and-down travelling shape. A displacement-distance sine graph can represent either type, so the graph shape alone does not establish particle direction. Compare the oscillation direction with the energy-transfer direction.','Explain the difference between transverse and longitudinal waves.','Transverse oscillations are perpendicular to propagation.~Longitudinal oscillations are parallel to propagation.')
add('4.1-08','Speed, frequency, period, wavelength and amplitude describe different aspects of a wave.','A displacement-distance graph gives spatial information, while a displacement-time graph gives temporal information.','Measure wavelength between neighbouring points in the same phase on a spatial snapshot; measure period between equivalent states at one position over time. Amplitude is measured from equilibrium, not from crest to trough. Keeping the horizontal-axis quantity clear prevents reading metres as seconds.','Explain how a displacement-distance graph and a displacement-time graph give different wave quantities.','Link the axes: neighbouring spatial crests give wavelength on the distance graph.~Equivalent successive peaks at one point give period on the time graph.')
for key,core,detail,teach,prompt,answer in [
('4.1-08-M01','Wave speed is the distance a disturbance travels per time.','Its SI unit is m/s.','Track a crest or pulse between two positions over a measured interval. Do not use the local up-down speed of a rope particle as the propagation speed.','Define wave speed.','It is the distance travelled by a wave disturbance per unit time.'),
('4.1-08-M02','Frequency is the number of complete oscillations per second.','Its unit is hertz, Hz.','Count complete cycles over a suitable time and divide by the duration. Ten cycles in two seconds gives 5 Hz. This is not the time for one cycle.','Define frequency.','Frequency is the number of complete oscillations per second.'),
('4.1-08-M03','Period is the time for one complete oscillation.','Its SI unit is the second.','Timing several cycles and dividing by their number can reduce the relative effect of reaction-time uncertainty. Check that each count represents a full return to the same state.','Define period.','Period is the time taken for one complete oscillation.'),
('4.1-08-M04','Wavelength is the distance between adjacent points in the same phase.','It can be measured between neighbouring crests or neighbouring compressions.','Crest to trough is half a wavelength for a simple sinusoidal wave, not a full wavelength. Measure along propagation, not along the curved length of a rope.','Define wavelength.','Wavelength is the distance between adjacent points in the same phase.'),
('4.1-08-M05','Amplitude is the maximum displacement from equilibrium.','Crest-to-trough displacement is twice amplitude for a symmetric sinusoidal wave.','Amplitude describes the size of oscillation, not its period or wavelength. A wave can have small amplitude and high frequency independently. Label the undisturbed level before measuring it.','Define amplitude.','Amplitude is the maximum displacement from equilibrium.')]:add(key,core,detail,teach,prompt,answer)
add('4.1-09','Wave speed, frequency and wavelength are linked by c = fλ.','Since f = 1/T, the same relation can be written c = λ/T.','One period advances a repeating wave pattern by one wavelength. This links a spatial interval with a time interval. A period in seconds cannot be inserted directly as though it were a frequency in hertz. Convert to a reciprocal or use the equivalent wavelength-over-period form.','Explain how wave speed can be obtained from wavelength and period.','Link one complete period to propagation by one wavelength.~Therefore c = λ/T, equivalent to c = fλ with f = 1/T.',heading='Wave equations')
add('4.1-09-M01','Wave speed is frequency multiplied by wavelength.','Use hertz and metres for m/s.','A 5 Hz ripple with wavelength 0.040 m has speed 0.20 m/s. If wavelength is supplied in centimetres, convert first. For a fixed wave speed, a higher source frequency gives a shorter wavelength. This requires a medium and regime where the speed is effectively unchanged.','Calculate the speed of a 6 Hz wave with wavelength 0.050 m.','c = fλ = 6 × 0.050.~The speed is 0.30 m/s.')
add('4.1-09-M02','Frequency and period are reciprocals.','f = 1/T when T is in seconds and f is in hertz.','A period of 0.25 s corresponds to 4 Hz. To find period from frequency, use T = 1/f. Shorter period means more cycles fit into a second. State the unit after taking the reciprocal because the new quantity is not a time.','Calculate the frequency for a period of 0.20 s.','f = 1/T = 1/0.20.~The frequency is 5 Hz.')
add('4.1-10','A wavefront joins adjacent points in the same phase.','A ray indicates propagation normal to a wavefront in the simple uniform-medium model.','Successive crest fronts are separated by a wavelength. A wavefront is not the path of one water particle. In a plan view of a ripple tank, circular fronts indicate spreading from a point source and straight parallel fronts indicate plane waves.','Define a wavefront.','It is a line joining adjacent points in the same phase.')
add('4.1-11','A point source produces circular wavefronts in a uniform water region; a straight source can produce plane fronts.','Propagation arrows are perpendicular to the local fronts.','Use a plan view and show several successive fronts at equal intervals for a steady source and speed. Circular fronts have increasing radii about one centre. Plane fronts are parallel straight lines. Their spacing represents wavelength, not amplitude; plan-view front drawings do not show vertical water height.','Explain how circular and plane wavefront drawings represent propagation.','Link the front geometry to direction: circular fronts spread radially from a point.~Plane fronts travel perpendicular to parallel straight lines.')
add('4.1-11-M01','Circular water wavefronts are concentric curves about a point source.','Equal radial spacing represents a constant wavelength in a uniform region.','Draw three circles of radii 1, 2 and 3 arbitrary length units about one marked source. Add radial outward arrows perpendicular to the curves. Do not show separate circles with unrelated centres or take circumference as wavelength.','Draw three successive circular crest wavefronts from a point source in uniform water, showing propagation direction.','Draw concentric circles with equal radial spacing.~Add outward radial arrows perpendicular to the fronts.')
A.geometry('4.1-11-M01',[dict(points=[[0,0],[3.5,0]],arrow=True)],circles=[dict(centre=[0,0],radius=r) for r in [1,2,3]],labels=[[0,.15,'Source'],[2,-.4,'Outward']],title='Circular wavefronts: plan view')
add('4.1-11-M02','Plane wavefronts are parallel straight lines.','A constant wavelength is shown by equal perpendicular spacing.','A straight vibrating bar in a ripple tank can generate an approximately plane wave train. Show the propagation arrow normal to the lines, not along them. The length of each drawn line is chosen for visibility and is not the wavelength.','Draw three plane crest wavefronts of equal wavelength, with a propagation arrow.','Draw three equally spaced parallel lines.~Add an arrow perpendicular to them in the propagation direction.')
A.geometry('4.1-11-M02',[dict(points=[[x,-2],[x,2]]) for x in [0,1,2]]+[dict(points=[[-.5,0],[3,0]],arrow=True,color='tab:red')],labels=[[2.2,.3,'Propagation']],title='Plane wavefronts: plan view')
add('4.1-12','Water-wave reflection changes propagation direction at a boundary.','Refraction from a depth change can alter speed, wavelength and direction while source frequency stays fixed.','At a straight barrier, incident and reflected ray angles measured from the normal are equal. Into shallower water, the usual ripple-tank wave speed falls; at unchanged frequency its wavelength shortens. Oblique incidence bends the propagation toward the normal. At normal incidence the spacing changes without a direction change.','Explain the different changes shown by reflection and refraction of water wavefronts.','Link reflection to a direction change with equal incident and reflected angles.~Refraction at a depth boundary changes speed and spacing while preserving the source frequency.')
add('4.1-12-M01','Reflected plane wavefronts obey equal incidence and reflection angles.','Ray directions are normal to fronts and are measured relative to the barrier normal.','Draw a horizontal barrier with a vertical normal at the reflection point. An incident ray travels down-right and the reflected ray up-right at equal normal angles. Place fronts perpendicular to each ray, keeping their spacing equal in the same water region. Arrows must show the incident ray toward the barrier and reflected ray away.','Draw incident and reflected rays and short wavefronts at a straight water-wave barrier for an incidence angle of 30° to the normal.','Draw the normal perpendicular to the barrier and rays at equal 30° angles on opposite sides of it.~Draw equally spaced fronts perpendicular to the rays, with arrows toward and away from the barrier.')
segs=[dict(points=[[-3,0],[3,0]],color='black'),dict(points=[[0,-.3],[0,3]],style='--',color='grey'),dict(points=[[-1.5,2.598],[0,0]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,0],[1.5,2.598]],arrow=True)]
for y in [1,2]:
 for sign in [-1,1]:
  x=sign*y/math.sqrt(3);segs.append(dict(points=[[x-.55,y+sign*.55/math.sqrt(3)],[x+.55,y-sign*.55/math.sqrt(3)]],color='tab:orange'))
A.geometry('4.1-12-M01',segs,labels=[[-3,-.4,'Barrier'],[.1,2.8,'Normal'],[-2,2.8,'Incident'],[1.5,2.8,'Reflected']],title='Reflection: rays normal to wavefronts')
add('4.1-12-M02','Into shallower water, ripple wavefront spacing becomes smaller at fixed frequency.','For oblique incidence, the ray bends toward the boundary normal.','Show closer front spacing on the shallow side and a smaller ray-normal angle. The fronts are perpendicular to their rays. A frequency label must stay unchanged across the boundary because the same source generates both trains. The direction change is absent for normal incidence, although the spacing still changes.','Draw wavefronts and ray directions for water waves entering shallower water obliquely, showing the wavelength change.','Draw the boundary and its normal, with the refracted ray closer to the normal.~Draw closer-spaced wavefronts perpendicular to the refracted ray, keeping frequency unchanged.')
segs=[dict(points=[[-3,0],[3,0]],color='black'),dict(points=[[0,-3],[0,3]],style='--',color='grey'),dict(points=[[-1.5,2.598],[0,0]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,0],[.75,-2.905]],arrow=True)]
for x,y,slope in [(-1,1.732,.577),(-.5,.866,.577),(.15,-.581,.258),(.30,-1.162,.258),(.45,-1.743,.258)]:segs.append(dict(points=[[x-.5,y-.5*slope],[x+.5,y+.5*slope]],color='tab:orange'))
A.geometry('4.1-12-M02',segs,labels=[[-2.8,2.5,'Deeper: longer spacing'],[-2.8,-2.5,'Shallower: shorter spacing']],title='Refraction: fixed frequency, lower speed')
A.misconception('MIS-15','Period and frequency are reciprocals rather than interchangeable inputs.','Wave speed can be calculated as wavelength divided by period.','For a wave with period 0.20 s and wavelength 0.060 m, frequency is 5 Hz and speed is 0.30 m/s. Multiplying 0.20 by 0.060 treats a time as a cycle rate and gives the wrong quantity and unit. Dimensional checks expose this conceptual mismatch.','A learner multiplies wavelength 0.060 m by period 0.20 s to find wave speed. Explain the error and correct result.','The error is treating period as frequency; f = 1/0.20 = 5 Hz.~The correct speed is c = λ/T = 0.30 m/s.')
C=A.choice
C('4.1-01','What is a pulse?',('A single disturbance','This distinguishes it from a repeated wave train.'),('A repeated train by definition','A pulse is single.'),('A fixed material object','A disturbance propagates.'),('The distance between crests','That is wavelength.'))
C('4.1-07','In a transverse wave, oscillations are oriented how?',('Perpendicular to propagation','This defines transverse.'),('Parallel to propagation','That defines longitudinal.'),('Without any motion','Oscillation is present.'),('Only toward the source','They alternate about equilibrium.'))
C('4.1-08-M05','Which distance gives amplitude?',('Equilibrium to maximum displacement','This is its definition.'),('Crest to trough','That is twice amplitude for a symmetric wave.'),('Crest to next crest','That is wavelength.'),('Source to observer','That is a travel distance.'))
C('4.1-10','What do points on a wavefront share?',('Phase','The front joins adjacent equal-phase points.'),('The same source distance in every wave type','Plane fronts need no single finite source centre.'),('A fixed temperature label','That is not the defining property.'),('A material path','A front is not one particle’s trajectory.'))
C('4.1-04','Which is a typical vacuum light speed?',('3.0 × 10⁸ m/s','This is the school approximation.'),('340 m/s','This is typical air sound speed.'),('1500 m/s','This is typical water sound speed.'),('5 m/s','This is far too slow.'))
C('4.1-03','What does an ideal rope wave mainly transfer along the rope?',('Energy','Rope elements oscillate locally.'),('The whole rope material continuously','That is not the wave model.'),('New rope mass','No matter is created.'),('Only a colour change','The disturbance is mechanical.'))
C('4.1-12-M02','Which remains unchanged at a stationary depth boundary?',('Source frequency','The same source drives both regions.'),('Wavelength necessarily','It changes with speed.'),('Speed necessarily','Depth can change it.'),('Direction for every incidence','Oblique refraction can bend the ray.'))
C('4.1-09-M01','For f = 4 Hz and λ = 0.08 m, what is speed?',('0.32 m/s','Multiply frequency and wavelength.'),('50 m/s','This divides f by wavelength.'),('0.02 m/s','This divides wavelength by frequency.'),('3.2 m/s','This introduces a factor of ten.'))
C('4.1-09-M02','A period is 0.5 s. What is frequency?',('2 Hz','Take the reciprocal.'),('0.5 Hz','Period and frequency differ.'),('5 Hz','This misreads the decimal.'),('0.25 Hz','Squaring the period is inappropriate.'))
C('4.1-06','With sound speed 340 m/s, what distance corresponds to an approximately 2 s light-sound delay?',('680 m','Sound distance is speed times delay.'),('170 m','This divides by time.'),('340 m','This ignores the 2 s interval.'),('0.006 m','This reverses time and speed.'))
C('4.1-08-M04','Four complete wavelengths span 20 cm. What is one wavelength?',('5 cm','Divide total distance by four.'),('80 cm','This multiplies instead.'),('20 cm','This ignores the number of intervals.'),('4 cm','This substitutes the number of waves as a length.'))
C('4.1-12-M02','A ripple enters shallower water and slows at unchanged frequency. What happens to wavelength?',('It decreases','λ = c/f.'),('It increases','That reverses the speed dependence.'),('It must stay fixed','Frequency alone does not fix wavelength.'),('It becomes amplitude','These are different measures.'))
P=A.part
P('4.1-08-M02','State the unit of frequency.','Hz.',1,'One mark for hertz.')
P('4.1-08-M03','State the meaning of period.','Time for one complete oscillation.',1,'One mark for a complete-cycle time.')
P('4.1-03','Explain the motion of a marked rope point as a transverse wave passes.','It oscillates across the propagation direction. The disturbance and energy travel along the rope without net transport of that marked element.',2,'One mark for transverse local motion; one for energy propagation without net medium transport.')
P('4.1-09-M01','Calculate speed for f = 8 Hz and λ = 0.025 m.','c = fλ = 0.20 m/s.',2,'One mark for fλ; one for 0.20 m/s.')
P('4.1-09-M02','Calculate period at 8 Hz.','T = 1/f = 0.125 s.',2,'One mark for reciprocal; one for 0.125 s.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot wavelength vertically against reciprocal frequency horizontally at fixed depth.',unit='m/s',meaning='The gradient is wave speed because λ = c(1/f). This interpretation assumes approximately unchanged speed over the tested range.',method=['Set a ripple tank to a measured uniform depth.','Drive plane waves at a known frequency and wait for a stable pattern.','Measure the distance across several neighbouring crest intervals and divide by the interval count.','Repeat for several frequencies while keeping depth fixed.','Repeat measurements and use a fixed projected scale or calibrated imaging to reduce reading uncertainty.'],control='Keep depth and geometry fixed, avoid reflections contaminating the reading and separate low-voltage equipment from spills.',table='Record frequency / Hz, reciprocal frequency / s, multi-interval distance / m, interval count and wavelength / m.',conclusion='A straight wavelength-versus-reciprocal-frequency line supports approximately constant wave speed; its slope gives that speed.')
